#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below, continuous at $x=0$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/5a83932fdde3fa50945ddea33311a58d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0$, it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x)$, plotted below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/d83d24b9d8babe8e669ffa5fe0213550.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0$, $x=1$, and $x=2.5$. It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/fd121626f4469d5b13b86e80782627ee.png)\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n2. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2$.\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n$lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)|= 2,; lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)|= 1$.\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function breaks at $x=2$. It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499855ad-f2de-5bd5-aa3a-e1e0312d1100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242124} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ee97ae-fba1-59ff-8520-8be5947cf413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242125} {:answer/id #uuid "974e1e94-75aa-51ef-ac38-60c44abfe821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242126} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8d8d83-d77a-5608-aa19-4cf4da602885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242127} {:answer/id #uuid "e4586339-52ea-5a00-b9b1-e687d62000a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8d8d83-d77a-5608-aa19-4cf4da602885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242127} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4a4d0a-1b4a-5c41-8cff-1321f5bd08c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d542cfea-06a6-5d32-836f-df32a05c8681" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-102115" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/d1d91b2d791d69c512a6bd2e7d523563de076a7f3afda53513234e799152ef8d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a break at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/57415045f7a1cc1c896756ff19cb17f90074151a25d12c56068fb6f86a064753.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2461a9a-00f6-5be0-9ed3-839c0ef2f7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242131} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e68ff7-cd9b-53e7-8723-9491fd8e5981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242132} {:answer/id #uuid "4d017967-67a8-5284-aea4-33b8812e0064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242133} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8273b6-f88e-5709-9736-64ca8bf45091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242134} {:answer/id #uuid "530a0b2b-48e2-58aa-a195-650051d3d7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2461a9a-00f6-5be0-9ed3-839c0ef2f7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242131} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a760ee7-ff47-57da-8065-07a5c45bec8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7d34789-19c3-5674-9b35-4b0229a653d1" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-104020" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/4c351342c55982f064955a311f9ea94220d58675e85c2e3514a12a8798f03df0.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7b6207-bdc9-5682-8424-abf4bb22813d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answ