 #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3d5f3e1-0f7f-56ba-9eea-f720be7a7122" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48038" :question/problem "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/3c8a198c1ec835590a3d755b964929ef9ac9a30e4268b2fdc80dacdb394899d8.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99974464-05f0-5484-b5c8-5071961c2a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067248} {:answer/id #uuid "a2704f5a-4561-5560-84de-cc643e4f8fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067249} {:answer/id #uuid "e54d547a-d72f-5400-b663-6dabc5245dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067250} {:answer/id #uuid "31f05587-9d13-5056-b680-2fa23737c8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067251} {:answer/id #uuid "3a19e16e-f1b4-540a-9711-0b044898f17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e54d547a-d72f-5400-b663-6dabc5245dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067250} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac6a1214-4886-5a61-a16d-89ac85f65b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71e6c6e3-bd2d-56df-b5ac-8bd9bdcce5d6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48039" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x =-2$." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below. Despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=1$, the graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/63e7a219c0c9bf7917a094ac0e65e9d4f18eb855476830621700d58ac29ba1b0.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=-2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac0290d8-8fd9-5534-bc72-ffd9bc1891ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067254} {:answer/id #uuid "d9aa2e2e-2f84-5bbb-acd8-18927828ab6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067255} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf038c8-4d9d-5b7b-91a9-f5332dc9ac49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067256} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb741e1-c93d-5bab-9b16-64fe9fec2999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067257} {:answer/id #uuid "525dd184-2719-54f9-a8bf-6850709a6d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "525dd184-2719-54f9-a8bf-6850709a6d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067258} :answer-field/id #uuid "30227c08-7b7c-52de-be3c-e21b64c8b023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544abde9-c223-5348-b980-33df85581d2d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48042" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48042.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\n1. $lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)$ is finite\n2. $lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)$ is finite\n3. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x = 2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdac98e0-36e0-5794-ba57-4e657325937a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242145} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d2ac6f-083c-5660-a925-a9787e203145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242146} {:answer/id #uuid "272ec425-9242-5a79-9ad8-c143c9075d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242147} {:answer/id #uuid "1449ee10-eb98-542c-9545-5d919b90c800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242148} {:answer/id #uuid "8a246f2e-217f-56a7-b085-efdb384f8745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a246f2e-217f-56a7-b085-efdb384f8745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242149} :answer-field/id #uuid "793146a7-3cfb-5660-ae6f-4074c81b3907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e8acf5a-125d-50fa-aa94-a610aec40435" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48062" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/d9db7f2a491ae3065dbe8434a71d378c4fed8fec16c82e2fd76dcb209c786809.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae053f5-e4f4-55d9-b9cd-61c178e0fdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} {:answer/id #uuid "ae177b99-76c0-5809-b534-565659d9f233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242153} {:answer/id #uuid "43587f4c-613a-5924-a3f9-7cbc29861063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242154} {:answer/id #uuid "8baf4ab7-cfb1-5cc0-8b8f-5982e1480435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242155} {:answer/id #uuid "4a223d96-5d5f-5da0-a49c-34bed0fb01a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8baf4ab7-cfb1-5cc0-8b8f-5982e1480435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242155} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b58d48d-3efa-5ed9-a245-2027d2c2df7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dd081ea-7bc3-509b-a198-9f265bd70e42" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe9a9f6e-f5ff-5242-aaf3-f2b62aca4ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242159} {:answer/id #uuid "882ba019-67b7-5632-9d36-dc04ddd2889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242160} {:answer/id #uuid "278f20a3-a2fd-557b-ad81-1d8c6b33abff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242161} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ee433-face-5659-8fd8-bd7103d8627d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242162} {:answer/id #uuid "2651fabb-2170-5a82-a67a-63514524d652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ee433-face-5659-8fd8-bd7103d8627d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242162} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbffbd34-9408-5b03-a562-a6d25528c228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "363cd399-4622-5aad-887a-00e756e2ba2e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48067" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/c132c73b7ca4759882959f9df42110c36e31f3c8af27afa02c0caa20f5f94d52.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f836507c-6151-54ae-9e4d-daf48edb255b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242166} {:answer/id #uuid "d128c3db-b502-5f30-815c-661d1997e153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242167} {:answer/id #uuid "afc92a17-14a7-5b6f-8840-3969b42a787c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242168} {:answer/id #uuid "5db6d7c3-7318-50d6-9300-693b9c2fa834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242169} {:answer/id #uuid "dd30b332-a8e2-58b7-a9eb-c691cc080975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d128c3db-b502-5f30-815c-661d1997e153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242167} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d91affc-5743-5eb3-94df-5d6dd7e4d49a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e7e4b6e-27ce-5625-a268-b7922ab3b080" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48068" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/8a3874b7907227653d93451d9ce0960fdadc8cb3e10396e0f27f7ebb363bebe2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n- $f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :