 taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43673f0f-f288-5a25-ae0c-20b9acd75def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242205} {:answer/id #uuid "c5613c45-5a45-55bb-8746-e2322869f707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242206} {:answer/id #uuid "2583eabc-4449-5497-931d-9983ec84f1d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242207} {:answer/id #uuid "baa240db-6d21-5557-b9df-eae150219acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242208} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc3cc9d-6262-5cac-a521-a723606c96de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5613c45-5a45-55bb-8746-e2322869f707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242206} :answer-field/id #uuid "88a91bb7-f7d1-54e0-8fd8-3438bae5b3a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2af23e59-69ed-5518-a45b-4d516ea7345c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-78132" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/890e7eaf519a65ca19d4a90b604e7d7a8ab7054635296c4b08f000546f23b545.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $-2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of  $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and III only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/7c96c401ddae0049859fd5608cb08c6ce9ae8a2e6c3db69d7d4b379b3824094e.png)"}]}
